{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791573873,"nonce":"jMrw9eD/VjQUMIN/Bup6FSvulmc=","message":"Item 3 landed in 7cdc8f8, with blinc_abi ff3faa2 (GraphContext host effects) and Blinc 0e4ffada. CI pins ff3faa2 and is green.\n\n**What changed**\n- Effects are host effects. Native code holds only each effect's key and its tag (the effect's id in the context's callback table). Nothing calls into JavaScript for an effect.\n- notify, endBatch, endEffect and effect creation return how many effects are due (no allocation); the context takes their tags into a reused Uint32Array with takeDue, and runs them in one pass, each between beginEffect and endEffect. Inside a batch or a run, the native side skips collecting due effects.\n- Writes made during a run apply when the outermost run ends, so an effect that writes what it read is due again and runs again in the same pass; a pass stops an effect after 1024 reruns. Every due effect runs and the first error is thrown after the pass. A failing first run leaves no effect behind, as before. An effect released during its own run still closes its scope: the native side keeps the open runs by key.\n- Computeds keep the per-context dispatcher.\n\n**Measured** (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, release addons. \"Before\" is 82f2a09 built in a separate worktree with its own lock, Blinc d4485d9 and closure effects; \"after\" is 7cdc8f8 with Blinc 0e4ffada. Two runs each.)\n\nbenchmarks/reactive-flush.mjs (median of 31; \"fan-out\" is one write that N effects read; \"batch\" is N writes in a batch, one effect reading each):\n- fan-out, N = 1,000: before 1.88 / 1.69 ms (about 1.8 µs per effect); after 1.06 / 1.04 ms (1.05 µs)\n- fan-out, N = 10,000: before 130.8 / 132.7 ms (13 µs per effect, growing with N); after 10.8 / 11.1 ms (1.1 µs)\n- batch, N = 1,000: before 0.78 / 0.81 ms (0.8 µs per write and effect); after 1.31 / 1.34 ms (1.3 µs)\n- batch, N = 10,000: before 7.77 / 7.61 ms (0.77 µs); after 13.3 / 14.3 ms (1.3-1.4 µs)\nThe fan-out gain is most likely Blinc's linear subscriber edits rather than host effects; the before build has the old Blinc. Per effect run, host effects cost more than one native-to-JS call did: a raw loop (begin, one track, end) costs about 850 ns per effect, and a single track about 260 ns, mostly napi-rs class-method overhead (this unwrap, type tag, borrow guard) per call. Folding end-of-one and begin-of-next into one call, or moving the graph methods off the napi class onto plain functions, are the next things to try.\n\nbenchmarks/reactive-memory.mjs, effect, 100,000 items (footprint per item alive [V8 heap]; then after six make/dispose rounds):\n- before: 1150 [389]; 1056, 1448, 1610, 1792, 1807, 1809 and 1152 [389]; 1143, 1438, 1616, 1804, 1810, 1791\n- after: 1266 [389]; 1339, 2146, 2172, 2172, 2173, 2174 and 1281 [389]; 1346, 2154, 2178, 2180, 2183, 2184\nSo per-effect memory did not drop; alive it is about 120 B higher and the rounds settle about 350 B higher. An intermediate version that returned a fresh Uint32Array from each call measured 1414-1432 alive, so per-call allocations show up here; the remaining difference has not been pinned down (candidates: GraphContext's tag HashMap and the new Blinc effect bookkeeping).","files":null}]}